{
  "image_name": "793ef01a28921931a3086517be5b2774.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure explains how high-entropy and other multi-component alloys develop strong lattice distortion and how that affects their properties. Across cubic and hexagonal crystal types, conventional alloys made of one main element are shown as regular, undistorted lattices, whereas five-component alloys contain differently sized atoms that visibly strain the lattice. A second schematic shows that unequal bonding strengths between unlike atoms can also distort atomic positions, adding to the disorder.\n\nThe right side connects this distorted structure to material behavior. In a pure metal, a dislocation moves relatively easily through a regular array of atoms. In a binary alloy, some resistance appears, and in a high-entropy alloy the mixed atomic environment creates a larger drag force, implying stronger resistance to dislocation motion and therefore strengthening. A similar comparison is made for electrons: transport is smoother in an undistorted alloy, but in a highly distorted multi-component alloy the electron path becomes more scattered.\n\nThe bottom schematic suggests a catalysis application. A mixed-metal surface provides many chemically distinct sites, and molecules such as CO2 appear to adsorb and be converted into products including CH4 and C2H4, highlighting the tunable reactivity of these complex alloy surfaces.",
